# Oh! Susanna, strum and sing (repertoire plan: oh-susanna). Stephen Foster's song of 1848 in G,
# the first verse and the chorus only, in modern spelling. The original second verse is never used.
# Your part: G (320003), C (x32010) and D7 (xx0212), two strums in every bar of 2/4: down on
# beat 1, down-up on beat 2. The verse sits on G with D7 at the ends of lines; the chorus starts
# on C ("Oh! Susanna"). Words are written above the bar where each line starts.
# The tune (second track) as it is commonly sung, in first position on strings 3, 2 and 1
# (G A B C D E): one pickup note ("I"), then "come from Al-a-bam-a" with the quick high E on the
# second "a"; each line ends on A (verse line 1, "knee") or G ("see", and the last "knee").
# "Going" is sung on one note, as in "I'm goin' to Louisiana".
title: Oh! Susanna
id: oh-susanna
composer: Stephen Foster
source: Song published in 1848; first verse and chorus; arranged for Sixstring Academy
arranger: Sixstring Academy
licence: Public domain (Foster died in 1864); this arrangement is original to Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 3
tags: strumming, 2/4, pickup, lyrics, g, c, d7
tempo: 92
time: 2/4
key: G
pickup: e

track: Your strum
role: rhythm
